<!-- A preliminary version of this exercise was drafted by Weidai Sun after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->
<!-- Chrissy Safranski provided a major rewrite in spring 2026 -->

<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="P" from="410" to="620" step="10"/>
  <select name="h"> 3 4 5 6 </select>

    <rectangle name="r1" center="(-4,0)" width="2" height="10" styleNumber="5" fixed/>
    <rectangle name="r2" center="(-2,0)" width="2" height="10" styleNumber="5" fixed/>
    <rectangle name="r3" center="(0,0)" width="2" height="10" styleNumber="5" fixed/>
    <rectangle name="r4" center="(2,0)" width="2" height="10" styleNumber="5" fixed/>
    <rectangle name="r5" center="(4,0)" width="2" height="10" styleNumber="5" fixed/>
    <rectangle name="r6" center="(6,0)" width="2" height="10" styleNumber="5" fixed/>

    <function name="A"> x*($P - 2*x)/($h+1) </function>
    <derivative name="AP"> $A </derivative>
    <number name="crNum"> $P/4 </number>
    <number name="max"> $$A($crNum) </number>
    <number name="vert"> $P/($h+1) - 2/($h+1)*$crNum </number>


  <conditionalContent name="group1">
    <case condition="$h = 3"> $r1 $r2 $r3 </case>
    <case condition="$h = 4"> $r1 $r2 $r3 $r4 </case>
    <case condition="$h = 5"> $r1 $r2 $r3 $r4 $r5 </case>
    <case condition="$h = 6"> $r1 $r2 $r3 $r4 $r5 $r6 </case>
  </conditionalContent>

</setup>

<p>
  A rancher wants to enclose a rectangular area and then divide it into <m>$h</m> equally-sized individual pens with fencing parallel to one side of the rectangle (see the figure below). Call the horizontal length <m>x</m> and the vertical length <m>y</m>.
</p>
<p>
  We want to answer the following question: if there are <m>$P</m> feet of fencing available to complete the job, what is the largest possible combined area of the <m>$h</m> pens?
</p>

  <graph name="g" aspectRatio="4" ymax="12" displayXAxis="false" displayYAxis="false" showNavigation="false" showBorder="false">
    <shortDescription>an image showing the equally-sized pens</shortDescription>
    $group1
    <label fixed anchor="(-6,0)"><m>y</m></label>
    <label fixed anchor="($h-5,9)"><m>x</m></label>
    <lineSegment fixed endpoints="(-6.5, -5) (-6.5,5)" styleNumber="5" />
    <lineSegment fixed endpoints="(-6.7, -5) (-6.3,-5)" styleNumber="5" />
    <lineSegment fixed endpoints="(-6.7, 5) (-6.3,5)" styleNumber="5" />
    <lineSegment fixed endpoints="(-5, 11) (2*$h-5,11)" styleNumber="5" />
    <lineSegment fixed endpoints="(-5, 10.2) (-5,11.8)" styleNumber="5" />
    <lineSegment fixed endpoints="(2*$h-5, 10.2) (2*$h-5,11.8)" styleNumber="5" />
  </graph>

<p>
  First, express the total area of the pen in terms of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
</p>
<p>
  <answer><label><m>A=</m></label>x*y</answer>
</p>

<p>
  The constraint is that we have <m>$P</m> feet of fencing available. Find an expression involving <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> for the total length of fencing needed to construct the pens, and use this to complete the constraint equation below.
</p>
<p>
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>$P=</m></label>2x+($h+1)y</answer>
</p>
<p>
  Next, solve the constraint equation for <m>y</m> in terms of <m>x</m>.
</p>
<p>
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>y=</m></label>($P-2x)/($h+1)</answer>
</p>
<p>
  Now we can recall that we want to optimize the total area of the pens, so we use the expression for <m>y</m> from the constraint equation to write <m>A</m> as a function of a single variable, <m>x</m>.
</p>
<p>
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>A(x)=</m></label>$A</answer>
</p>
<p>
  We know that the absolute maximum (and minimum) can only occur at a critical number or an endpoint.  Thus, we find the derivative of <m>A</m> and use it to find the critical number(s); if there is more than one critical number, separate them with commas.  If there are none, enter <q>none</q>.
</p>
<p>
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>A'(x)=</m></label>$AP</answer>
</p>
<p>
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label>The critical number(s) of <m>A</m> are <m>x=</m></label>$crNum</answer>
</p>
<p>
  Since <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are lengths, they can't be negative.  That means that the smallest possible value of <m>x</m> is <answer name="xmin"><label><m>x=</m> </label>0</answer> and the maximum possible value for <m>x</m> is when <m>y=0</m>, which implies <answer name="xmax" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label> <m>x=</m></label>$P/2</answer>, so
         <mathInput disabled><label>the domain of <m>A</m> in interval notation is</label>[<conditionalContent condition="$xmin.creditAchieved=1">$xmin</conditionalContent>,<conditionalContent condition="$xmax.creditAchieved=1">$xmax</conditionalContent>]</mathInput>.
      </p>
<p>
  In both cases, when either <m>x=0</m> or <m>y=0</m>, the area <answer><label><m>A=</m></label>0</answer>.  This is the absolute minimum area that can be enclosed with the provided fence.
</p>

<p>
  The absolute maximum of <m>A</m> occurs when <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>x=</m></label>$crNum</answer> and the absolute
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label>maximum enclosed area is </label>$max</answer>
with <answer><choiceInput inline><label>units</label>
  <choice>?</choice>
  <choice>feet</choice>
  <choice credit="1">square feet</choice>
  <choice>cubic feet</choice>
</choiceInput></answer>.
</p>
<p>
  <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label>The horizontal length of one of the individual pens is </label>$crNum/$h</answer> feet, and the vertical length of one of the individual pens is <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>y=</m></label>$vert</answer> feet.
</p>
